.TH REWIND 1 "October 2026" "rewind 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rewind \- universal undo for destructive shell commands
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rewind
.I COMMAND
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rewind
intercepts destructive shell commands, stages their pre\-action state, runs
the real command with unchanged output and exit status, and keeps a local
journal so the action can be reversed later.
.PP
The wrapped commands are
.BR rm ,
.BR mv ,
.BR cp ,
.BR chmod ,
.BR chown ,
and the destructive git operations
.BR reset ,
.BR clean ,
.BR checkout ,
.BR restore ,
and
.BR branch \fR(\fB\-d\fR/\fB\-D\fR).
Interception is performed by shell functions installed by
.BR "rewind init" ;
the binary itself can also be invoked directly with
.BR "rewind exec" .
.PP
Staged data lives in a per\-user trash directory. Undo is resumable: items
restored successfully are removed from the journal immediately, so a partial
failure can be repaired and retried without re\-restoring completed items.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B undo \fR[\fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
Reverse the last \fIN\fR journal entries (default 1), newest first. With
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR, print what would be restored without changing anything.
.TP
.B log \fR[\fB\-n\fR \fILIMIT\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
Show undo history. The default limit is 30 entries. JSON output includes the
full command, working directory, status, exit code, item count, and staged
bytes.
.TP
.B inspect \fIID\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR]
Inspect one journal entry, its staged items, and recovery readiness without
restoring anything. With \fB\-\-json\fR, emit machine\-readable JSON.
.TP
.B status \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR]
Show data locations, undo stack depth, retention settings, enabled wrappers,
and whether a shell hook was found.
.TP
.B clean \fR[\fB\-\-older\-than\fR \fIDURATION\fR] [\fB\-\-all\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
Purge staged files older than the retention period, or enforce the configured
size cap. Durations accept the suffixes s, m, h, d, and w. History rows are
kept and marked \fBexpired\fR.
.TP
.B init \fR{\fBbash\fR|\fBzsh\fR|\fBfish\fR}
Print the shell integration script to standard output. Typical installation:
.PP
.RS
.nf
eval "$(rewind init bash)"            # ~/.bashrc
eval "$(rewind init zsh)"             # ~/.zshrc
rewind init fish | source             # ~/.config/fish/config.fish
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B completions \fR{\fBbash\fR|\fBzsh\fR|\fBfish\fR|\fBelvish\fR|\fBpowershell\fR}
Print shell completions to standard output.
.TP
.B config \fR{\fBpath\fR|\fBshow\fR|\fBget\fR \fIKEY\fR|\fBset\fR \fIKEY\fR \fIVALUE\fR|\fBinit\fR|\fBedit\fR}
Inspect or change configuration. Boolean values accept
\fItrue/false\fR, \fIyes/no\fR, \fIon/off\fR, and \fI1/0\fR.
.TP
.B exec \fICOMMAND\fR \fB\-\-\fR \fIARGS\fR...
Execute a wrapped command. This is the entry point used by the shell hooks and
is hidden from the normal help output.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration is optional. Defaults enable every wrapper and keep trash for
30 days. The file is read from
.I ~/.config/rewind/config.toml
(or \fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR/rewind/config.toml).
.PP
Recognized keys:
.PP
.RS
.nf
wrap.rm, wrap.mv, wrap.cp, wrap.chmod, wrap.chown, wrap.git
wrap.git_reset, wrap.git_clean, wrap.git_checkout, wrap.git_restore, wrap.git_branch_delete
trash.retention_days (0 disables automatic expiry)
trash.max_bytes (0 means unlimited)
journal.keep_days (0 keeps history forever)
git.snapshot_untracked, git.keep_refs
.fi
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B REWIND_DISABLE
When set to \fB1\fR, \fBtrue\fR, or \fByes\fR, the shell hook and
.B rewind exec
pass commands directly to the real binary.
.TP
.B REWIND_DATA_DIR
Override the data directory (journal and trash).
.TP
.B REWIND_JOURNAL
Override the journal database path.
.TP
.B REWIND_CONFIG_PATH
Override the configuration file path.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Base for the default data directory (\fI~/.local/share/rewind\fR).
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base for the default configuration path (\fI~/.config/rewind\fR).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/rewind/journal.db
SQLite journal of wrapped commands and staged items.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/rewind/trash/
Per\-entry staged data, created with mode 0700.
.TP
.I ~/.config/rewind/config.toml
Optional configuration.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B rewind
itself exits 0 on success, 1 when an undo is incomplete or a command fails,
and 2 for command\-line usage errors. Wrapped commands preserve their native
exit status. \fBrewind undo\fR exits 1 if any entry could not be fully
restored.
.SH SAFETY
rewind refuses to stage or delete its own data directory or any ancestor
thereof. Undo refuses to overwrite a path that has been recreated since the
command ran; the conflict is reported and the entry stays in the journal.
When staging is impossible, the command runs natively and is recorded as
non\-undoable rather than being blocked.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Non\-UTF\-8 arguments, unmodeled flags such as \fBrm \-\-one\-file\-system\fR,
branch switching, interactive git modes, and submodule internals are passed
through without undo.
Deleted files occupy trash until
.B rewind clean
runs.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rm (1),
.BR mv (1),
.BR cp (1),
.BR chmod (1),
.BR chown (1),
.BR git (1)
.PP
Full documentation: https://github.com/janghotan/rewind
